<exercises xml:id="ex-AlgebraicManipulations">
    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Use polynomial division or some clever factoring to rewrite and find the following indefinite integrals or evaluate the following definite integrals.
            </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>\dint \left(\dfrac{x+4}{x-3}\right)\;dx</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>\int \left(\dfrac{x+4}{x-3}\right)\;dx \amp  = \int 1 + \frac{7}{x-3}\;dx</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = x + 7\ln|x-3|+C</mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>\dint \left(\dfrac{x^2+4}{x-4}\right)\;dx</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>\int \left(\dfrac{x^2+4}{x-4}\right)\;dx \amp \int x + 4 + \frac{20}{x-4}\;dx</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = \frac{x^2}{2} + 4x + 20\ln|x-4|+C</mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>\dint \left(\dfrac{t^2+t+6}{t^2+1}\right)\;dt</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>\int \left(\dfrac{t^2+t+6}{t^2+1}\right)\;dt \amp = \int 1 + \frac{t+5}{t^2+1}</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = \int 1 \;dt + \int \frac{t}{t^2+1}\;dt + \int \frac{5}{t^2+1}\;dt</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = t + \frac{1}{2}\ln(t^2+1) + 5\tan^{-1}(t)+C</mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>\dint_{x=2}^{x=4}\left(\dfrac{x^3+1}{x-1}\right)\;dx</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>\int_{x=2}^{x=4}\left(\dfrac{x^3+1}{x-1}\right)\;dx \amp = \int_{x=2}^{x=4} x^2 + x + 1 + \frac{2}{x-1}\;dx</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = \left(\frac{x^3}{3} + \frac{x^2}{2} + x + 2\ln|x-1|\right)\bigg|_{x=2}^{x=4}</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = \frac{80}{3} + 2\ln(3)</mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>\dint_{x=0}^{x=1} \left(\dfrac{x^4+1}{x^2+1}\right)\;dx</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>\int_{x=0}^{x=1} \left(\dfrac{x^4+1}{x^2+1}\right)\;dx \amp = \int_{x=0}^{x=1} x^2-1 + \frac{2}{x^2+1}\;dx</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = \left( \frac{x^3}{3} - x + 2\tan^{-1}(x) \right)\bigg|_{x=0}^{x=1}</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = \frac{\pi}{2} - \frac{2}{3}</mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
    </exercise>
    <exercise xml:id="problem-CompleteTheSquare">
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Complete the square in order to find the following indefinite integrals.
            </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>\dint\left( \dfrac{1}{x^2-2x+10} \right)\;dx</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>\int\left( \dfrac{1}{x^2-2x+10} \right)\;dx \amp = \int \left( \frac{1}{(x-1)^2 + 9}\right)\;dx</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = \frac{1}{3}\tan^{-x}\left(\frac{x-1}{3}\right)+C</mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>\dint\left( \dfrac{x}{x^2+4x+8} \right)\;dx</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <hint>
                <p>
                    Try a <m>u</m>-substitution first! The degree in the numerator is one lower than the degree in the denominator, so it's a great candidate for using <m>u=x^2+4x+8</m>.
                </p>
            </hint>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>u \amp = x^2+4x+8 </mrow>
                        <mrow>du \amp = (2x+4) \;dx</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = 2(x+4)\;dx</mrow>
                    </md>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>\int\left( \dfrac{x}{x^2+4x+8} \right)\;dx \amp = \frac{1}{2}\int\left( \dfrac{2((x+4)-4)}{x^2+4x+8} \right)\;dx</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = \frac{1}{2}\int \frac{2(x+4)}{x^2+4x+8}\;dx - 4\int \frac{1}{x^2+4x+8}\;dx</mrow>
                    </md>
                    We can think of these two integrals separately.
                    <md>
                        <mrow>\frac{1}{2}\int \frac{2(x+4)}{x^2+4x+8}\;dx  \amp = \frac{1}{2} \int \frac{1}{u}\;du</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = \frac{1}{2}\ln|u|+C</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = \frac{1}{2}\ln(x^2+4x+8)+C</mrow>
                    </md>
                    Now we can complete the square in the denominator of the second integral.
                    <md>
                        <mrow> 4\int \frac{1}{x^2+4x+8}\;dx \amp = 4\int \frac{1}{(x+2)^2+4}\;dx </mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = 2\tan^{-1}\left(\frac{x+2}{2}\right)+C</mrow>
                    </md>
                    So, combining these, we have:
                    <md>
                        \int\left( \dfrac{x}{x^2+4x+8} \right)\;dx = \frac{1}{2}\ln(x^2+4x+8) - 2\tan^{-1}\left(\frac{x+2}{2}\right)+C
                    </md>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>\dint \left( \dfrac{2x}{x^4+6x^2+10} \right)\;dx</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <hint>
                <p>
                    Try to complete the square first, and then see if you can use a <m>u</m>-substitution afterwards.
                </p>
            </hint>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow> \int \left( \dfrac{2x}{x^4+6x^2+10} \right)\;dx \amp = \int \left(\frac{2x}{(x^2+3)^2+1}\right)\;dx</mrow>
                        <mrow>u =  \amp x^2+3</mrow>
                        <mrow>du \amp = 2x\;dx</mrow>
                        <mrow>\int \left(\frac{2x}{(x^2+3)^2+1}\right)\;dx \amp = \int \frac{1}{u^2+1}\;du</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = \tan^{-1}(u)+C</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = \tan^{-1}(x^2+3)+C</mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
    </exercise>
    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Find the following indefinite integrals.
            </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>\dint\left(\dfrac{1}{x^{-1}+1}\right)\;dx</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>\int\left(\dfrac{1}{x^{-1}+1}\right)\;dx \amp = \int\left(\dfrac{1}{x^{-1}+1}\right)\left(\frac{x}{x}\right)\;dx</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = \int \frac{x}{1+x}\;dx</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = \int 1 - \frac{1}{1+x}\;dx</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = x - \ln|1+x| + C</mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>\dint\left(\dfrac{\sin(\theta)+\tan(\theta)}{\cos^2(\theta)}\right)\;d\theta</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>\int\left(\dfrac{\sin(\theta)+\tan(\theta)}{\cos^2(\theta)}\right)\;d\theta \amp = \int \left(\frac{\sin(\theta)}{\cos^2(\theta)} + \frac{\tan(\theta)}{\cos^2(\theta)}\right)\;d\theta </mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = \int \left( \frac{\sin(\theta)}{\cos^2(\theta)} + \frac{\sin(\theta)}{\cos^3(\theta)} \right) \; d\theta</mrow>
                        <mrow>u \amp = \cos(\theta)</mrow>
                        <mrow>du \amp = -\sin(\theta)\;d\theta</mrow>
                        <mrow>\int \left( \frac{\sin(\theta)}{\cos^2(\theta)} + \frac{\sin(\theta)}{\cos^3(\theta)} \right) \; d\theta \amp = \int -u^{-2} - u^{-3}\;du </mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = \frac{1}{3u^3} + \frac{1}{4u^4} + C</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = \frac{1}{3\cos^3(\theta)} + \frac{1}{4\cos^4(\theta)} + C</mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>\dint\left(\dfrac{1-x}{1-\sqrt{x}}\right)\;dx</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>\int\left(\dfrac{1-x}{1-\sqrt{x}}\right)\;dx \amp = \int\left(\dfrac{1-x}{1-\sqrt{x}}\right)\left(\frac{1+\sqrt{x}}{1+\sqrt{x}}\right)\;dx</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = \int \frac{(1-x)(1+\sqrt{x})}{1-x}\;dx</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = \int 1+\sqrt{x}\;dx</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = x + \frac{2x^{3/2}}{3}+C</mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>
            </solution>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>\int\left(\dfrac{1-x}{1-\sqrt{x}}\right)\;dx \amp = \int \frac{(1-\sqrt{x})(1+\sqrt{x})}{1-\sqrt{x}}\;dx</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = \int 1+\sqrt{x}\;dx</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = x + \frac{2x^{3/2}}{3}+C</mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>\dint\left(\dfrac{1}{1-\sin^2(\theta)}\right)\;d\theta</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow> int\left(\dfrac{1}{1-\sin^2(\theta)}\right)\;d\theta \amp = \int \left(\frac{1}{\cos^2(\theta)}\right)\;d\theta</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = \int \sec^2(\theta)\;d\theta</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = \tan(\theta)+C</mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>\dint\left( \dfrac{x^{2/3}-x^3}{x^{1/4}} \right)\;dx</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>int\left( \dfrac{x^{2/3}-x^3}{x^{1/4}} \right)\;dx \amp = \int \left( \frac{x^{2/3}}{x^{1/4}} - \frac{x^3}{x^{1/4}} \right) \; dx</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = \int x^{5/12} - x^{11/4} \; dx</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = \frac{12x^{17/12}}{17} - \frac{4x{15/4}}{15} + C</mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>\dint\left(\dfrac{4+x}{\sqrt{1-x^2}}\right)\;dx</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>\int\left(\dfrac{4+x}{\sqrt{1-x^2}}\right)\;dx \amp = \int \frac{4}{\sqrt{1-x^2}}\;dx + \int \frac{x}{\sqrt{1-x^2}}\;dx</mrow>
                    </md>
                    Let's consider each integral separately.
                    <md>
                        \int \frac{4}{\sqrt{1-x^2}}\;dx = 4\sin^{-1}(x)+C
                    </md>
                    And we'll use a <m>u</m>-substitution for the second one, with <m>u=1-x^2</m> and <m>du = -2x\;dx</m>.
                    <md>
                        <mrow> \int \frac{x}{\sqrt{1-x^2}}\;dx \amp = -\frac{1}{2} \int \frac{1}{\sqrt{u}}\;du</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = -\frac{1}{2}\int u^{-1/2}\;du</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = -u^{1/2}+C</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = -\sqrt{1-x^2}+C</mrow>
                    </md>
                    So combining these, we have:
                    <md>
                        \int\left(\dfrac{4+x}{\sqrt{1-x^2}}\right)\;dx = 4\sin^{-1}(x) - \sqrt{1-x^2} + C
                    </md>
                    
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
    </exercise>
</exercises>